{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019hN27KUcSSACE6fxbRg75g","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"combined_score\": 0.027080121099549548,\n  \"correct\": true,\n  \"entrypoint_path\": \"src/program.py\",\n  \"program_dir\": \"work/results/t10/claude/20261111_094019/investigation/ideas/01_cross_fitted_recalibrated_ppi_intervals/program_1\",\n  \"source_attempt_id\": \"cycle01_crppi_mean_ci_attempt_01\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-11-11T10:41:09.181793+00:00\"\n}Cross-fitted isotonic/binned recalibration of Yhat as an extra control variate over PPI++ gives small, family-dependent width gains (largest on synthetic miscalibrated predictors, moderate on gene expression and forest, near zero where Yhat is already near-linearly calibrated, e.g. galaxies), with pooled coverage slightly below nominal, so aggressive small-margin switching between candidates is risky. Label-free smooth hinge-basis control variates with leave-one-out model-size selection and leverage-inflated variance were much weaker than monotone step/bin calibrators (nearly no gain even on synthetic miscalibration), so that route is unpromising: the inflation and selection costs outweigh the extra flexibility. Promising next lead: reduce the variance of the monotone/binned calibrator itself, through non-negative cross-validated stacking of several calibrators and repeated cross-fitting with a split-dispersion variance term, always nested with PPI++ as fallback and with stack weights fit only on training folds. Unpromising: extra labels, active sampling, or predictor changes are unavailable under the fixed-input setting.\nresults/task:\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\ntask.md\n\ntask:\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\nresources\ntask.md\ntask_contract.json\n@Article{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI,\n@Article{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP,\n@Article{Zrnic2023CrosspredictionpoweredI,\n@Article{Liu2024CreditRP,\n@Arti