{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw5hePumn3mPtU2HJQXi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LSmcVrL3ZMkh9DgfcKKB4u","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nstrategy = {\n \"status\":\"ready\",\n \"selected_prompt_family\":\"paper_standalone\",\n \"recommended_title\":\"Recalibrating the Control Variate in Prediction-Powered Mean Intervals: Small, Regime-Dependent Width Gains over PPI++ with Fixed Labels, Unlabeled Data, and Predictor\",\n \"central_question\":\"When the labeled set, unlabeled set and predictor are all fixed, can a cross-fitted recalibration of the predictor output (added as a second control variate to PPI++) shorten a valid confidence interval for a population mean, by how much, and in which regimes does it help or fail?\",\n \"central_thesis\":\"Adding a cross-fitted monotone/binned recalibration g(Yhat) as an extra control variate nested around PPI++ gives a small but measurable average narrowing of mean intervals (about 2% in geometric-mean width on held-out sealed pools, ~7% vs PPI lambda=1, ~30% vs classical), concentrated where the predictor is miscalibrated (census income, plankton, miscalibrated synthetic) and near zero where the raw prediction is already well calibrated, with empirical coverage held at a level the evaluator's exact binomial tests did not reject but whose point estimates (pooled 0.934, minimum family 0.91) sit below the nominal 0.95.\",\n \"narrative_rationale\":\"The completed final evaluation is outcome=qualified (final.qualification.outcome), status=completed, correct=true, claim_status_code=2 (narrower than PPI++ with coverage not rejected, by a paired bootstrap lower bound on the aggregate score), so a conditional-support paper is grounded. The task itself welcomes conditional answers, and the strongest honest story is a bounded one: the PPI++ interval is not the shortest valid interval, but the headroom reachable with only (Y, Yhat) on the fixed inputs is small and regime dependent, which matches the efficiency-bound view (gain = drop from Var(Y - lambda*Yhat) to Var(Y - E[Y|Yhat])). Search and final are kept separate: three model variants were all scored in search (no unscored attempts), the frozen one was evaluated once on a disjoint sealed set of pools. Coverage point estimates below 0.95 and the absence of ablations/CIs on width ratios are central limitations and must be foregrounded, not hidden. No downstream_study block exists in paper_data, so none is used.\",\n \"prior_paper_story_status\":\"rejected\",\n \"edit_framing_decision\":\"Edit mode is not active (no previous_paper, no edit context files); a fresh framing was chosen. Candidate framings compared: (a) 'new method beats PPI++' headline - rejected because gains are ~2% and coverage is below nominal in point estimate; (b) 'label-free spline basis / stacked calibration as the contribution' - rejected because those variants scored lower or comparable in search and the stacked variant did not beat the simpler one; (c) conditional/limits paper centred on recalibrated control variates plus an efficiency-bound analysis of when gain is possible - selected.\",\n \"staged_input_impact\":[\"No staged prior manuscript, new-program or old-program inputs are present; framing derives only from paper_data.json final_evaluation, the three scored search attempts, the task contract/evaluators and the frozen program source.\"],\n \"method_groups\":[\n  {\"name\":\"Classical labeled-only interval and PPI (lambda=1)\",\"role\":\"baseline\",\"include_reason\":\"Reference points that the evaluator reports directly (final mean log width ratio 0.358 vs classical and 0.070 vs PPI lambda=1, positive = narrower); show how much of the total gain over labeled-only inference is already captured by existing PPI machinery.\"},\n  {\"name\":\"PPI++ (power-tuned scalar lambda on raw Yhat)\",\"role\":\"baseline\",\"include_reason\":\"The primary comparator for every width ratio and the score; also the fallback nested inside the proposed interval.\"},\n  {\"name\":\"Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI (isotonic and quantile-binned g(Yhat); [Yhat, g] as control variates with variance-minimising weights; Student-t critical value; adopt the recalibrated interval only if its half-width is at least 1% below PPI++)\",\"role\":\"proposed\",\"include_reason\":\"The frozen search winner and the method evaluated in the sealed final; the paper's studied object.\"},\n  {\"name\":\"Stratified PPI (quantile bins of Yhat, lambda=1 per stratum)\",\"role\":\"supporting\",\"include_reason\":\"Implemented in the frozen program as a comparison estimator and relevant prior work; include only as a method description/related work unless evaluator-reported numbers exist for it (none appear in paper_data: do not report numeric results for it).\"},\n  {\"name\":\"Search-stage alternative variants: label-free hinge-basis control variates with PRESS selection; repeated cross-fitted NNLS-stacked calibration\",\"role\":\"ablation\",\"include_reason\":\"Two scored alternative designs give a design-space contrast (search combined scores 0.0046 and 0.0223 vs 0.0271 for the winner). Present as a single-evaluation design comparison on search pools, not as controlled ablations, and without uncertainty claims.\"}\n ],\n \"results_focus\":[\n  \"Sealed final evaluation (5 families, each 100 independent labeled/unlabeled draws, n=500, N=20000 for the four real pools; synthetic mixes four regimes, n in {200,500}, N in {2000,20000}): per-family log width ratio vs PPI++ (census_income 0.0287, census_healthcare 0.0018, census_education 0.0023, plankton 0.0359, synthetic 0.0413), aggregate 0.0220; convert to width reductions (1-exp(-s)) as derived values and say so.\",\n  \"Aggregate narrowing vs the three comparators: 0.022 (PPI++), 0.070 (PPI lambda=1), 0.358 (classical) mean log ratios; make clear that most of the gain over labeled-only inference comes from PPI itself and recalibration adds a small increment.\",\n  \"Validity reporting: pooled coverage 0.934 and minimum-family coverage 0.91 vs nominal 0.95; evaluator gate (exact one-sided binomial tests, pooled level 0.01, per-family Bonferroni) not rejected, controls (constant predictor, permuted predictor, shift) passed, 0 failed episodes. State that the point estimates are below nominal and the gate is a non-rejection, not proof of nominal coverage; per-family coverage other than the minimum is not available.\",\n  \"Where gain exists vs not: gains largest on synthetic (nonlinear miscalibrated regime is the designed case), plankton and census_income; near zero on census_healthcare and census_education. Do not attribute causes beyond what the analytic argument and predictor-calibration reasoning support; no per-regime ablation was run, so mechanisms are hypotheses unless a derivation backs them.\",\n  \"Stability across five evaluator partitions of the final run (partition scores 0.0203, 0.0213, 0.0220, 0.0235, 0.0247; derived range only; these are reset-seed partitions of one evaluator protocol, not independent method trainings, and candidate diagnostics trust is 'unverified'); report as spread, not as a confidence interval.\",\n  \"Search-stage design contrast on disjoint search pools (galaxies, forest, gene_expression, alphafold, synthetic; scored attempts 3 of 3, none unscored): winner 0.0271, stacked-calibration 0.0223, label-free basis 0.0046; per-family log ratios vs PPI++ for the winner: alphafold 0.0104, forest 0.0168, galaxies 0.0035, gene_expression 0.0290, synthetic 0.0757. Search coverage pooled 0.943 / min family 0.933 for the winner; the stacked variant had min family 0.917. Label the contrast as single evaluations; separate it clearly from final results.\",\n  \"Analytic section: asymptotic variance with control variates [Yhat, g]; PPI++ is nested (lambda on Yhat only), recalibrated gain governed by reduction from Var(Y - lambda*Yhat) to residual variance of the best g, bounded below by Var(Y - E[Y|Yhat]) when only Yhat is used; list assumptions (iid labeled/unlabeled from one population, fixed predictor, mean estimand, alpha=0.05, finite second moments, cross-fitted g at adequate rate) and no-gain conditions (already-linear calibration, uninformative predictor, small n). State explicitly that a numeric theory-vs-observed width-ratio check is not part of the available artifacts and was not performed.\"\n ],\n \"figure_plan\":[\n  \"Fig. 1 (early, page 1-2): conceptual overview - fixed inputs (n labeled pairs, N unlabeled predictions, frozen predictor) -> three interval constructions on the same data (classical; PPI++ with scalar lambda on Yhat; recalibrated PPI with cross-fitted g(Yhat) as an additional control variate and a 1%-margin fallback to PPI++) -> evaluation by coverage and width ratio vs PPI++; annotate the efficiency-bound idea (headroom = Var(Y-lambda*Yhat) down to Var(Y-E[Y|Yhat])).\",\n  \"Fig. 2: sealed final results - panel (a) per-family width reduction vs PPI++ (5 bars, positive = narrower); panel (b) aggregate mean log ratio against PPI++, PPI lambda=1, classical (3 bars). Coverage goes in a small table next to it, not a figure.\",\n  \"Fig. 3: search-stage design contrast - grouped bars of per-family log width ratio vs PPI++ for the three model variants across five search families, with the coverage-minimum shown in a table or annotation; explicitly 'single evaluation per variant'.\",\n  \"Table 1: final summary (per-family log ratio and derived width ratio; pooled and min coverage; controls; failed episodes; partition score range). Table 2: method/estimator comparison (what each interval uses, assumptions).\"\n ],\n \"figure_specs\":[\n  {\"figure_id\":\"overview_setting_and_method\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Glance-level view of the fixed-input problem, the nested interval constructions, and the evaluation protocol, so the reader sees what is being shortened and relative to what.\",\"figure_type\":\"diagram\",\"preferred_generator\":\"generate-concept-figure\",\"group_by\":\"method_family\",\"metric\":\"none (conceptual)\",\"split_by\":\"none\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":0.95,\"layout_rationale\":\"ICLR template is single-column (text width about 5.5 in); a left-to-right schematic with 3 stages and short labels needs the full text width to keep labels at >= 8 pt; place on page 1 or 2.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":1,\"max_categories_per_panel\":6,\"max_series_per_panel\":4},\n  {\"figure_id\":\"final_width_ratio\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Show the sealed-final width narrowing vs PPI++ by family and vs the three comparators in aggregate, with the zero-gain line visible.\",\"figure_type\":\"grouped_bar\",\"preferred_generator\":\"matplotlib\",\"group_by\":\"family\",\"metric\":\"log width ratio vs comparator (positive = narrower), plus derived percent reduction\",\"split_by\":\"panel (per-family vs aggregate)\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":0.95,\"layout_rationale\":\"Two side-by-side panels (5 bars and 3 bars) with family-name tick labels; full text width keeps tick labels horizontal and legible; do not shrink to column width.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":2,\"max_categories_per_panel\":5,\"max_series_per_panel\":1},\n  {\"figure_id\":\"search_variant_contrast\",\"required\":True,\"purpose\":\"Contrast the three scored model variants on search pools to show which design choices (monotone/binned recalibration vs smooth label-free basis vs stacked calibration) carry the gain; kept separate from final results.\",\"figure_type\":\"grouped_bar\",\"preferred_generator\":\"matplotlib\",\"group_by\":\"search_family\",\"metric\":\"log width ratio vs PPI++ (positive = narrower)\",\"split_by\":\"none\",\"preferred_span\":\"full_width\",\"target_width_fraction\":0.95,\"layout_rationale\":\"5 categories x 3 series in one panel needs full text width for readable legends; single panel avoids density problems.\",\"allow_progression\":False,\"max_panels\":1,\"max_categories_per_panel\":5,\"max_series_per_panel\":3}\n ],\n \"supplementary_plan\":{\n  \"purpose\":\"Provide reimplementation-level and protocol detail plus the full numeric tables behind the main figures, without relocating evidence essential to the central claim.\",\n  \"planned_subsections\":[\n   {\"heading\":\"Estimator specification and hyperparameters\",\"grounded_content\":\"Exact form of the frozen interval: PPI++ closed-form lambda clipped to [0,1]; 5-fold cross-fitted isotonic (direction by correlation sign) and quantile-binned g (bins = min(10, max(2, floor(0.8n/25)))); [Yhat, g] standardised control variates with ridge-stabilised variance-minimising weights; Student-t critical value with df = n-1-p via Cornish-Fisher; 1% half-width margin rule vs PPI++; classical fallback on non-finite output. Taken from the frozen program source.\",\"source_ids\":[\"result.best_correct_score\",\"result.top_program_01\"]},\n   {\"heading\":\"Evaluation protocol and gates\",\"grounded_content\":\"Sealed-final design from the task contract and evaluator: pools, n and N, 100 draws per family, synthetic regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated, uninformative, heteroscedastic) with n in {200,500} and N in {2000,20000}, score definition s_f = -log(sum widths / sum PPI++ widths), failed-episode penalty (miss, 10x classical width), exact binomial coverage gate, constant/permuted/shift controls, bootstrap-based claim status; truth = pool mean (analytic for synthetic).\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.qualification.outcome\",\"final.qualification.status\",\"final.metric.claim_status_code\",\"final.metric.controls_passed\",\"final.metric.n_failed_episodes\"]},\n   {\"heading\":\"Full final-evaluation tables\",\"grounded_content\":\"All per-family log ratios vs PPI++, mean log ratios vs PPI lambda=1 and classical, pooled/min coverage, five partition scores and durations, with derived width-ratio conversions.\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.metric.combined_score\",\"final.metric.coverage_pooled\",\"final.metric.coverage_min_family\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_census_income\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_census_healthcare\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_census_education\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_synthetic\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_lambda1_mean\",\"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_classical_mean\",\"final.evidence.episodes\"]},\n   {\"heading\":\"Search-stage variant details\",\"grounded_content\":\"Per-family search metrics and coverage for the three scored variants, description of the label-free basis/PRESS and stacked-calibration designs, and the fact that each was evaluated once (3 of 3 scored, none unscored).\",\"source_ids\":[\"result.top_program_01\",\"result.top_program_02\",\"result.top_program_03\",\"result.total_attempts\",\"result.total_evaluated_programs\"]},\n   {\"heading\":\"Derivation of the variance-reduction argument\",\"grounded_content\":\"Full derivation of the control-variate asymptotic variance, nesting of PPI++, efficiency-bound reading, listing of assumptions and no-gain conditions. Analytic content only; no numerical validation claimed.\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.metric.combined_score\"]},\n   {\"heading\":\"Reproducibility and LLM usage\",\"grounded_content\":\"Environment (ppi-python 0.2.3 baselines, numpy/scikit-learn), seeds visible in artifacts (final partitions 9101-9105), CPU-only ~266 s per partition, data-pool provenance, an anonymised LLM Usage statement required by the venue.\",\"source_ids\":[\"final.evidence.episodes\"]}\n  ]\n },\n \"forbidden_headings\":[\"Search Campaign\",\"Iteration History\",\"Progression of Results\",\"Milestones\",\"First Valid Result\",\"Cycle 1/2/3\",\"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\"Run Log\",\"What We Tried\"],\n \"forbidden_phrases\":[\"correctness rate\",\"correct and eligible\",\"combined_score\",\"rubric\",\"cycle01\",\"cycle02\",\"cycle03\",\"attempt_01\",\"program_1\",\"idea_id\",\"worker_01\",\"state of the art\",\"significantly outperforms\",\"achieves nominal coverage\",\"guaranteed coverage\",\"universally\",\"first attempt\",\"after several iterations\"],\n \"notes_for_writer\":[\n  \"Qualification facts come from final.qualification.*: outcome=qualified, status=completed, correct=true. Do not say the method failed qualification; also do not turn 'qualified' into a generalization or superiority claim. It means the evaluator found valid-by-gate coverage and an aggregate width gain over PPI++ whose paired-bootstrap lower bound was positive (claim_status_code=2); the bootstrap value itself is not in paper_data.\",\n  \"Be candid that pooled coverage 0.934 and min-family 0.91 are below 0.95; the gate is non-rejection with 500 pooled and 100 per-family draws. Do not claim nominal coverage was attained. Avoid inferring cause (Wald/t approximation, selection among candidates) unless supported by a derivation, and label it a hypothesis.\",\n  \"Search scores (single evaluation each, disjoint pools, smaller n/N such as forest n=100, N=1000) and final scores are different evaluations; never pool them or rank variants by search score differences as if statistically established. Winner-vs-stacked difference (0.0271 vs 0.0223) is small and has no uncertainty estimate.\",\n  \"Width differences are ratios of summed widths over a family's draws (not per-draw paired CIs); say so. Derived percentages (1-exp(-s)) are computations on reported values; present them as derived.\",\n  \"No ablation artifacts exist (evidence_sources.ablations is missing). Describe the isotonic/binned/fallback components, but do not claim component-level attributions; state which questions remain open (effect of K, bins, margin, t vs normal, n sweep, small-n regime).\",\n  \"The task also asked about other estimands and a labeled-size sweep; the artifacts cover only the mean estimand, alpha=0.05, with n=500 on real pools. Scope statements must reflect that; ballots and other pools were not part of final scoring and no real-data n sweep was scored.\",\n  \"Stratified PPI is in the frozen source but has no reported evaluator metrics; mention only as related/implemented baseline without numbers. Cite only keys present in references.bib (PPI Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI, PPI++ Angelopoulos2023PPIEP, cross-PPI Zrnic2023CrosspredictionpoweredI, stratified PPI Fisch2024StratifiedPI, and the clearly relevant Gu2024LocalPI, Li2025PredictionPoweredAS, Cortinovis2025FABPPIFA, Kluger2025PredictionPoweredIW, Hofer2024BayesianPI where genuinely related); do not cite unrelated entries (credit risk, cardiac, microbiome, soil, conformal ICL). Double-machine-learning, isotonic and control-variate literature is not in the bib; cite only if retrieved later, otherwise describe without citation.\",\n  \"Layout: ICLR single-column; target 8-9 body pages, last page >=75% full. Spend space on problem setup and fixed-input framing, the nested estimator definition, the analytic headroom argument, final results with Table 1, a failure/no-gain discussion, and limitations; not on search narrative. Fig.1 early; Figs 2-3 full text width; export PDF + 300-DPI PNG from source, reference the PDF in LaTeX; no raster upscaling.\",\n  \"Overview figure uses generate-concept-figure (only one such figure); the other figures use matplotlib.\",\n  \"Anonymity: no author names, no repository links; include LLM Usage section (appendix allowed).\",\n  \"Name variants reader-facingly (e.g. 'recalibrated PPI', 'basis-expansion PPI', 'stacked-calibration PPI'); use 'seeded runs' / 'evaluation partitions' rather than pipeline terms.\"\n ],\n \"failure_reason\":\"\"\n}\njson.dump(strategy,open('paper_strategy.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nEOF\npython3 -c \"import json;json.load(open('/workspace/paper/paper_strategy.json'));print('ok')\""},"call